<body lang="EN-US">
<div class="WordSection1">

<h1>Methods &mdash; NEURON Simulation Scripts</h1>
<div class="title-block">
<p><b>Project:</b> CloudvolNeuron_Test &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Author:</b> Juan
&nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Document date:</b> 17 May 2026</p>
<p><b>Repository path:</b>
<span class="code">\\wsl.localhost\ubuntu\home\jd\NeuronProject\CloudvolNeuron_Test</span></p>
<p class="muted">Technical lab-notebook write-up of the four Python scripts
used to build and exercise NEURON cable / ball-and-stick models in this
project. Each section documents purpose, inputs, outputs, internal
workflow and module dependencies. Numerical parameters are tabulated
with units and brief justifications.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================ INTRO ============================== -->
<h2>1. Overview</h2>
<p>The project contains four Python files that, taken together, form a
small NEURON tutorial pipeline. <span class="code">NeuronDemo.py</span>
introduces a single-compartment soma; <span class="code">BallStickDemo.py</span>
extends this to a two-compartment ball-and-stick neuron with Hodgkin&ndash;
Huxley kinetics; <span class="code">NonIsopotentialCable.py</span>
explores how a passive cylindrical cable&rsquo;s steady-state response
varies with stimulus amplitude, diameter, specific membrane resistance and
axial resistivity, comparing simulated outputs against analytical cable
theory; and <span class="code">MatplotlibVis.py</span> is a helper class
that renders any live NEURON morphology in a matplotlib 3D axis and can
display NEURON&rsquo;s native <span class="code">PlotShape</span> window
alongside it.</p>

<p>All four scripts target NEURON&rsquo;s modern Python interface
(<span class="code">from neuron import n</span> and
<span class="code">neuron.units</span>); the cable script also uses the
legacy <span class="code">h</span> interface where it is more concise.
The standard run library (<span class="code">stdrun.hoc</span>) is loaded
in every script before any
<span class="code">finitialize</span> / <span class="code">continuerun</span>
call. All electrical quantities follow NEURON&rsquo;s default conventions:
voltages in mV, currents in nA, time in ms, lengths in &mu;m, axial
resistivity in &Omega;&middot;cm, specific membrane resistance in
&Omega;&middot;cm<sup>2</sup>, specific capacitance in &mu;F/cm<sup>2</sup>,
and density-mechanism conductances in S/cm<sup>2</sup>.</p>

<h3>1.1 Common environment</h3>
<p>Every script can be run with the same Python environment. The union of
required third-party packages is:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>neuron</b> (&ge; 8.x; exposes both the <span class="code">h</span>
    and <span class="code">n</span> interfaces and the
    <span class="code">neuron.units</span> module).</li>
<li><b>matplotlib</b> (&ge; 3.x; <span class="code">pyplot</span> and
    <span class="code">mpl_toolkits.mplot3d</span> for the 3D viewer).</li>
<li><b>numpy</b> (vector ops, linear regression via
    <span class="code">polyfit</span>).</li>
<li><b>scipy</b> (only <span class="code">scipy.optimize.curve_fit</span>
    is imported in the cable script and is reserved for nonlinear fits).</li>
<li><b>pandas</b> and <b>plotnine</b> (data-frame plotting alternatives;
    imported in the tutorial scripts and kept for the optional
    persistence/visualisation blocks).</li>
</ul>
<p>Python standard-library modules used across the scripts:
<span class="code">csv</span>, <span class="code">json</span>,
<span class="code">pickle</span>, <span class="code">pprint</span>,
<span class="code">textwrap</span>, <span class="code">math</span>,
<span class="code">os</span>, <span class="code">sys</span>,
<span class="code">typing</span>.</p>

<!-- ====================== NeuronDemo.py ============================ -->
<h2>2. <span class="code">NeuronDemo.py</span> &mdash; single-compartment soma</h2>

<h3>2.1 Purpose</h3>
<p>An introductory NEURON walkthrough: build a one-section model whose
single cylinder represents an isolated soma, drive it with a sustained
current, integrate the membrane equation for 40&nbsp;ms, and demonstrate
how the resulting time / voltage trace can be persisted to disk in CSV,
JSON and pickle formats for later inspection.</p>

<h3>2.2 Inputs and outputs</h3>
<p><b>Inputs.</b> No external files are read by default. All parameters
are hard-coded constants set inside the script. When the optional
persistence/loading blocks are enabled, the script reads/writes the
sibling files <span class="code">data.csv</span>,
<span class="code">data.json</span> and
<span class="code">data.p</span>.</p>

<p><b>Outputs.</b> The script prints to stdout the NEURON version, the
pretty-printed <span class="code">soma.psection()</span> dictionary, and
the soma length / diameter as confirmation. When the matplotlib block is
enabled, an interactive figure of <i>v(t)</i> at
<span class="code">soma(0.5)</span> is shown. The optional persistence
block produces the following file formats:</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="code">data.csv</span> &mdash; two columns,
    <span class="code">t&nbsp;(ms)</span> and
    <span class="code">v&nbsp;(mV)</span>, no header.</li>
<li><span class="code">data.json</span> &mdash; a dictionary
    <span class="code">{"t":[&hellip;], "v":[&hellip;]}</span> with
    <span class="code">indent=4</span>.</li>
<li><span class="code">data.p</span> &mdash; a binary pickle of
    <span class="code">{"t": Vector, "v": Vector}</span>, which preserves
    the NEURON <span class="code">Vector</span> type on reload.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3 How the script works</h3>
<ol>
<li>Imports NEURON together with the unit constants
    <span class="code">ms</span>, <span class="code">mV</span>,
    <span class="code">um</span>.</li>
<li>Builds a single section <span class="code">soma</span> with
    <span class="code">L = diam = 20&nbsp;&mu;m</span> and prints its
    full <span class="code">psection()</span> dictionary.</li>
<li>Creates an <span class="code">IClamp</span> point process at
    <span class="code">soma(0.5)</span> with a 15&nbsp;ms / 0.9&nbsp;nA
    pulse starting at <i>t</i>=0.</li>
<li>Records <span class="code">soma(0.5).v</span> and the global
    simulation time into two NEURON
    <span class="code">Vector</span>s.</li>
<li>Loads <span class="code">stdrun.hoc</span>, initialises at
    &minus;65&nbsp;mV and integrates to 40&nbsp;ms.</li>
<li>Optionally serialises the recorded trace via CSV / JSON / pickle.</li>
</ol>

<h3>2.4 Parameter table</h3>
<table class="params">
<thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Units</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="col-param">soma.L</td><td class="col-value">20</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Length-equals-diameter cylinder approximating a small spherical soma; standard NEURON tutorial value.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">soma.diam</td><td class="col-value">20</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Surface area &asymp;1257&nbsp;&mu;m<sup>2</sup> &mdash; sufficient for an illustrative spike.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">iclamp.delay</td><td class="col-value">0</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Pulse begins at the start of the simulation so the entire response is captured.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">iclamp.dur</td><td class="col-value">15</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Long enough to elicit a clearly visible deflection given the model time-scale (40&nbsp;ms).</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">iclamp.amp</td><td class="col-value">0.9</td><td class="col-unit">nA</td><td class="col-just">Deliberately large drive so the passive soma reaches a substantial depolarisation in 15&nbsp;ms.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">finitialize</td><td class="col-value">&minus;65</td><td class="col-unit">mV</td><td class="col-just">Canonical resting potential used throughout the tutorial scripts.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">continuerun</td><td class="col-value">40</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Captures both the depolarising pulse and ~25&nbsp;ms of relaxation after pulse offset.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>2.5 Required modules</h3>
<p><span class="code">neuron</span> (with <span class="code">h</span>,
<span class="code">n</span> and <span class="code">neuron.units</span>),
<span class="code">matplotlib</span>, <span class="code">numpy</span>,
<span class="code">pandas</span>, <span class="code">plotnine</span>,
plus standard-library <span class="code">csv</span>,
<span class="code">json</span>, <span class="code">pickle</span>,
<span class="code">pprint</span>, <span class="code">textwrap</span>,
<span class="code">math</span>.</p>

<!-- ====================== BallStickDemo.py ========================= -->
<h2>3. <span class="code">BallStickDemo.py</span> &mdash; two-compartment ball-and-stick neuron</h2>

<h3>3.1 Purpose</h3>
<p>Construct a minimal ball-and-stick neuron consisting of a soma
(&ldquo;ball&rdquo;) and a passive dendrite (&ldquo;stick&rdquo;), inject
a brief current pulse at the dendritic tip, and observe how the
resulting voltage transient propagates back to the soma. The script then
sweeps the stimulus amplitude and the dendritic spatial discretisation
(<span class="code">nseg</span>) to illustrate how spatial resolution
influences the simulated trace; this is the
<i>nseg-convergence</i> exercise from the official NEURON Python
tutorial.</p>

<h3>3.2 Inputs and outputs</h3>
<p><b>Inputs.</b> None on disk. All parameters live in the script.</p>
<p><b>Outputs.</b> Console: NEURON version, unit string for
<span class="code">gnabar_hh</span>, and the list of density mechanisms
attached to every section, followed by a per-amplitude progress message
during the sweep. Graphics: a single matplotlib figure showing soma
(solid) and dendrite (dashed) voltage traces for each of the four
stimulus amplitudes overlaid for
<span class="code">nseg = 1</span> (thick) and
<span class="code">nseg = 101</span> (thin). No data files are written
by default.</p>

<h3>3.3 How the script works</h3>
<ol>
<li>Imports NEURON, matplotlib and <span class="code">neuron.gui</span>
    so that NEURON&rsquo;s native graphical primitives are registered (the
    interactive shape window is currently disabled because it does not
    behave reliably under WSL).</li>
<li>Loads <span class="code">stdrun.hoc</span>.</li>
<li>Defines a <span class="code">BallAndStick</span> class with two
    builder methods:
    <ul>
    <li><span class="code">setup_morphology()</span> creates the soma and
        dendrite, connects <span class="code">dend(0)</span> to
        <span class="code">soma(1)</span> and stores
        <span class="code">soma.wholetree()</span> in
        <span class="code">self.all</span> for later iteration.</li>
    <li><span class="code">setup_biophysics()</span> sets axial resistivity
        and specific capacitance on every section, inserts the
        Hodgkin&ndash;Huxley mechanism (<span class="code">hh</span>) in
        the soma with the classic squid-axon densities, and inserts the
        passive mechanism (<span class="code">pas</span>) in the dendrite
        with leak conductance 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>&nbsp;S/cm<sup>2</sup>
        and reversal &minus;65&nbsp;mV.</li>
    </ul></li>
<li>Instantiates one cell (<span class="code">my_cell</span>) and attaches
    an <span class="code">IClamp</span> at the distal end of the
    dendrite (<span class="code">dend(1)</span>).</li>
<li>Records voltage at the centres of soma and dendrite, plus global
    time, into NEURON Vectors.</li>
<li>Runs a baseline simulation (<span class="code">finitialize(&minus;65 mV)</span>
    &rarr; <span class="code">continuerun(25 ms)</span>) followed by a
    nested loop over four amplitudes and two
    <span class="code">nseg</span> values; for each (amp, nseg) the cell
    is re-initialised, integrated, and the resulting curves are
    appended to the matplotlib axes.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3.4 Parameter table</h3>
<table class="params">
<thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Units</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="col-param">soma.L</td><td class="col-value">12.6157</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Chosen so that &pi;&middot;d&middot;L &asymp; 500&nbsp;&mu;m<sup>2</sup>, matching a spherical soma of the same diameter (valid for electrical-only modelling).</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">soma.diam</td><td class="col-value">12.6157</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;m</td><td class="col-just">See above. L = diam keeps the soma roughly isopotential at default discretisation.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">dend.L</td><td class="col-value">200</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Comparable to a few space constants of the passive dendrite so attenuation toward the tip is visible.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">dend.diam</td><td class="col-value">1</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Representative of a thin distal dendritic shaft.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">Ra</td><td class="col-value">100</td><td class="col-unit">&Omega;&middot;cm</td><td class="col-just">Mid-range mammalian cytoplasmic resistivity (literature 70&ndash;200).</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">cm</td><td class="col-value">1</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;F/cm<sup>2</sup></td><td class="col-just">Canonical specific membrane capacitance for biological lipid bilayers.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">hh.gnabar</td><td class="col-value">0.12</td><td class="col-unit">S/cm<sup>2</sup></td><td class="col-just">Hodgkin&ndash;Huxley (1952) squid axon default.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">hh.gkbar</td><td class="col-value">0.036</td><td class="col-unit">S/cm<sup>2</sup></td><td class="col-just">Hodgkin&ndash;Huxley default.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">hh.gl</td><td class="col-value">3&times;10<sup>&minus;4</sup></td><td class="col-unit">S/cm<sup>2</sup></td><td class="col-just">Hodgkin&ndash;Huxley default leak.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">hh.el</td><td class="col-value">&minus;54.3</td><td class="col-unit">mV</td><td class="col-just">Hodgkin&ndash;Huxley default leak reversal.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">pas.g</td><td class="col-value">10<sup>&minus;3</sup></td><td class="col-unit">S/cm<sup>2</sup></td><td class="col-just">Rm = 1000&nbsp;&Omega;&middot;cm<sup>2</sup>, a typical passive value.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">pas.e</td><td class="col-value">&minus;65</td><td class="col-unit">mV</td><td class="col-just">Matches the chosen resting potential so the dendrite starts at zero passive current.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">stim.delay</td><td class="col-value">5</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Allows a 5&nbsp;ms baseline before the pulse.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">stim.dur</td><td class="col-value">1</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Brief pulse to elicit a single transient response without re-stimulating.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">stim.amp</td><td class="col-value">{0.075, 0.150, 0.225, 0.300}</td><td class="col-unit">nA</td><td class="col-just">Spans a 4&times; range, large enough to drive HH spiking at the higher amplitudes.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">dend.nseg</td><td class="col-value">{1, 101}</td><td class="col-unit">&mdash;</td><td class="col-just">Compares the default coarse discretisation against a clearly converged one. nseg is kept odd so the recording point at x = 0.5 lies inside a segment, not on a boundary.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">continuerun</td><td class="col-value">25</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Covers the 5&nbsp;ms pre-pulse baseline, the 1&nbsp;ms pulse and ~19&nbsp;ms of post-pulse dynamics.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>3.5 Required modules</h3>
<p><span class="code">neuron</span> (with <span class="code">n</span>,
<span class="code">neuron.gui</span> and <span class="code">neuron.units</span>),
<span class="code">matplotlib</span>, plus standard-library
<span class="code">os</span> and <span class="code">sys</span>. The
helper class <span class="code">NeuronMatplotlibVisualizer</span>
defined in <span class="code">MatplotlibVis.py</span> can be enabled by
uncommenting the corresponding import line; this is optional.</p>

<!-- =================== NonIsopotentialCable.py ===================== -->
<h2>4. <span class="code">NonIsopotentialCable.py</span> &mdash; passive cable parameter study</h2>

<h3>4.1 Purpose</h3>
<p>Numerically verify the predictions of <i>passive cable theory</i> for
a finite, non-isopotential cylindrical cable. The script performs four
parameter sweeps &mdash; on stimulus amplitude <i>I</i>, cable diameter
<i>d</i>, specific membrane resistance <i>R<sub>m</sub></i> and axial
resistivity <i>R<sub>a</sub></i> &mdash; and compares the peak voltage
deflection at the injection site and at the distal end of the cable
against the analytical expressions for the space constant and the
input resistance.</p>

<div class="callout">
<p><b>Key analytical relations (infinite cable):</b></p>
<p>&lambda; = &radic;(<i>R<sub>m</sub>&middot;d</i> / (4&middot;<i>R<sub>a</sub></i>))
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
<i>R</i><sub>in</sub> = &radic;(<i>R<sub>m</sub>&middot;R<sub>a</sub></i> /
(&pi;&middot;<i>d</i>))</p>
<p>So &lambda; &prop; &radic;<i>d</i>, &lambda; &prop; &radic;<i>R<sub>m</sub></i>,
&lambda; &prop; 1/&radic;<i>R<sub>a</sub></i>; and
<i>R</i><sub>in</sub> &prop; 1/&radic;<i>d</i>,
<i>R</i><sub>in</sub> &prop; &radic;<i>R<sub>m</sub></i>,
<i>R</i><sub>in</sub> &prop; &radic;<i>R<sub>a</sub></i>.</p>
</div>

<h3>4.2 Inputs and outputs</h3>
<p><b>Inputs.</b> None on disk. Hard-coded baseline cable parameters
(modified per sweep, see table below).</p>
<p><b>Outputs.</b> Console: NEURON banner, default-parameter echo, and
for every sweep step the (&lambda;, <i>R</i><sub>in</sub>) prediction
together with the simulated peak deflections
&Delta;<i>V</i><sub>center</sub> and &Delta;<i>V</i><sub>end</sub>;
each sweep finishes with the fitted regression slope. Graphics: four
2&times;3 matplotlib figures (one per sweep) containing voltage traces
at centre and end, a visualisation of the swept parameter or the
&lambda; relationship, and the &Delta;<i>V</i> linearisation overlaid
with theoretical and least-squares fits.</p>

<h3>4.3 How the script works</h3>
<ol>
<li>Builds a single passive cable section
    (<span class="code">h.Section(name="cable")</span>) with default
    parameters (table below).</li>
<li>Attaches an <span class="code">IClamp</span> at the geometrical
    centre of the cable.</li>
<li>Defines two helper functions:
    <span class="code">calculate_lambda(d, Rm, Ra)</span> and
    <span class="code">calculate_Rin_cable(d, Rm, Ra)</span>, both of
    which perform the unit conversion &mu;m&nbsp;&harr;&nbsp;cm
    internally.</li>
<li>Iterates four sweeps. For each step a cable property is updated, two
    recording <span class="code">Vector</span>s are bound to
    <span class="code">cable(0.5).v</span> and
    <span class="code">cable(1.0).v</span>, the model is initialised at
    <i>v</i><sub>rest</sub> = &minus;70&nbsp;mV and integrated to
    25&nbsp;ms, the peak deflections are appended, and the corresponding
    subplot is updated.</li>
<li>Linear regressions (<span class="code">numpy.polyfit</span>) and a
    theoretical line are overlaid in the appropriate column-3 subplot;
    R<sup>2</sup> is annotated for Section&nbsp;1. The first three
    sweeps call <span class="code">plt.show(block=False)</span> so
    figures stack; the final sweep ends with a blocking
    <span class="code">plt.show()</span>.</li>
</ol>

<h3>4.4 Baseline cable parameters</h3>
<table class="params">
<thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Units</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="col-param">cable.L</td><td class="col-value">1000</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Long compared to &lambda; (&asymp;160&nbsp;&mu;m for the default cable), so the finite-cable correction to <i>R</i><sub>in</sub> is small.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">cable.diam</td><td class="col-value">1</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Thin baseline; varied to 2, 4, 8&nbsp;&mu;m in Section&nbsp;2.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">cable.cm</td><td class="col-value">1.0</td><td class="col-unit">&mu;F/cm<sup>2</sup></td><td class="col-just">Canonical biological value.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">pas.g</td><td class="col-value">10<sup>&minus;3</sup></td><td class="col-unit">S/cm<sup>2</sup></td><td class="col-just">R<sub>m</sub> = 1000&nbsp;&Omega;&middot;cm<sup>2</sup>; varied in Section&nbsp;3.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">pas.e</td><td class="col-value">&minus;70</td><td class="col-unit">mV</td><td class="col-just">Equal to v<sub>rest</sub> so passive current is zero at rest.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">cable.Ra</td><td class="col-value">100</td><td class="col-unit">&Omega;&middot;cm</td><td class="col-just">Mid-range mammalian value; varied in Section&nbsp;4.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">v<sub>rest</sub></td><td class="col-value">&minus;70</td><td class="col-unit">mV</td><td class="col-just">Passive initial condition.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">iclamp.delay</td><td class="col-value">0</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Pulse begins at <i>t</i> = 0.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">iclamp.dur</td><td class="col-value">15</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Long enough that the membrane reaches near-steady-state (15&nbsp;ms &raquo; &tau;<sub>m</sub> = R<sub>m</sub>&middot;c<sub>m</sub> = 1&nbsp;ms).</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">iclamp.amp</td><td class="col-value">0.1</td><td class="col-unit">nA</td><td class="col-just">Small enough to stay safely in the linear (passive) regime.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">continuerun</td><td class="col-value">25</td><td class="col-unit">ms</td><td class="col-just">Captures the 15&nbsp;ms pulse plus ~10&nbsp;ms of relaxation.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>4.5 Sweep specifications</h3>
<table class="params">
<thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Swept parameter</th><th>Values</th><th>Theoretical scaling</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td class="col-param">iclamp.amp</td><td class="col-value">0.075, 0.150, 0.225, 0.300&nbsp;nA</td><td>&Delta;V &prop; <i>I</i> (Ohmic, linear).</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td class="col-param">cable.diam</td><td class="col-value">1, 2, 4, 8&nbsp;&mu;m</td><td>&lambda; &prop; &radic;<i>d</i>, &nbsp;&Delta;V &prop; 1/&radic;<i>d</i>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td class="col-param">pas.g (Rm)</td><td class="col-value">Rm = 500, 1000, 2000, 4000&nbsp;&Omega;&middot;cm<sup>2</sup></td><td>&lambda; &prop; &radic;<i>R<sub>m</sub></i>, &nbsp;&Delta;V &prop; &radic;<i>R<sub>m</sub></i>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td class="col-param">cable.Ra</td><td class="col-value">50, 100, 200, 400&nbsp;&Omega;&middot;cm</td><td>&lambda; &prop; 1/&radic;<i>R<sub>a</sub></i>, &nbsp;&Delta;V &prop; &radic;<i>R<sub>a</sub></i>.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>4.6 Required modules</h3>
<p><span class="code">neuron</span> (with both
<span class="code">h</span> and <span class="code">n</span> interfaces
and <span class="code">neuron.units</span>),
<span class="code">numpy</span>,
<span class="code">scipy.optimize</span> (reserved for future nonlinear
fits), <span class="code">matplotlib</span>,
<span class="code">pandas</span>, <span class="code">plotnine</span>,
plus standard-library <span class="code">csv</span>,
<span class="code">math</span>, <span class="code">pprint</span>,
<span class="code">textwrap</span>.</p>

<!-- ====================== MatplotlibVis.py ========================= -->
<h2>5. <span class="code">MatplotlibVis.py</span> &mdash; 3D morphology viewer</h2>

<h3>5.1 Purpose</h3>
<p>Provide a small, reusable helper class
(<span class="code">NeuronMatplotlibVisualizer</span>) that renders any
live NEURON model&rsquo;s morphology in a matplotlib 3D axis. The class
can also drive NEURON&rsquo;s native
<span class="code">PlotShape</span> window so both GUIs appear together.
This is convenient when working under WSL or other environments where
the native window alone is awkward.</p>

<h3>5.2 Inputs and outputs</h3>
<p><b>Inputs.</b> A live NEURON model in the same Python process (the
class iterates over <span class="code">n.allsec()</span> at draw time);
optionally a NEURON <span class="code">PlotShape</span> instance to
display alongside the matplotlib view.</p>
<p><b>Outputs.</b> A <span class="code">matplotlib.figure.Figure</span>
returned by <span class="code">plot()</span>; an interactive 3D
matplotlib window when <span class="code">show=True</span>; and (when
<span class="code">save_path</span> is given) a 150&nbsp;dpi image file
&mdash; PNG by default but determined by the supplied filename
extension.</p>

<h3>5.3 How the class works</h3>
<ol>
<li><span class="code">_read_section_points(sec)</span> returns four
    parallel lists of 3D coordinates and diameters for one section.
    It prefers the section&rsquo;s explicit
    <span class="code">n3d()</span> point list and falls back to
    <span class="code">psection()["morphology"]["pt3d"]</span>; if
    neither exists the section is silently skipped.</li>
<li><span class="code">plot(save_path, show, ps)</span> calls
    <span class="code">n.define_shape()</span> so any sections that
    were specified only by L and diam acquire a default 3D point
    list, then iterates over <span class="code">n.allsec()</span> and
    draws each segment as a line whose width scales with local
    diameter; a scatter marker is added at every 3D point.</li>
<li><span class="code">wrap_show()</span> /
    <span class="code">wrap_and_show()</span> store a reference to a
    NEURON <span class="code">PlotShape</span> so that a subsequent
    <span class="code">show_both()</span> call can open it alongside
    the matplotlib figure. The two-stage interface is required because
    NEURON&rsquo;s <span class="code">PlotShape</span> objects do not
    accept user-defined attributes.</li>
<li><span class="code">show_both()</span> opens the stored NEURON window
    and re-draws the matplotlib view, catching exceptions from either
    GUI so the call cannot leave the user with half a view.</li>
</ol>

<h3>5.4 Parameter table</h3>
<table class="params">
<thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Units</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="col-param">scale_lw</td><td class="col-value">0.05</td><td class="col-unit">multiplier on &mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Converts local diameter to matplotlib line width; keeps thick somata legible without dominating the axes.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">scatter_scale</td><td class="col-value">0.5</td><td class="col-unit">multiplier on &mu;m</td><td class="col-just">Marker area = (d &middot; scatter_scale)<sup>2</sup>; chosen empirically to be visible without overlapping for typical cell sizes.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">figsize</td><td class="col-value">(7, 6)</td><td class="col-unit">inches</td><td class="col-just">Square-ish figure that fits comfortably on standard laptop displays.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">savefig dpi</td><td class="col-value">150</td><td class="col-unit">dots / inch</td><td class="col-just">Print-quality default while keeping file size modest.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>5.5 Required modules</h3>
<p><span class="code">neuron</span> (imported lazily inside
<span class="code">plot</span> and
<span class="code">apply_to_plotshape</span>),
<span class="code">matplotlib</span> with
<span class="code">mpl_toolkits.mplot3d</span>,
<span class="code">numpy</span>, plus standard-library
<span class="code">typing</span>.</p>

<!-- ====================== Reproduction notes ======================= -->
<h2>6. Reproduction notes</h2>
<p>To reproduce any single figure from scratch, run the corresponding
script from the project root with the project&rsquo;s Python
environment:</p>
<pre>cd ~/NeuronProject/CloudvolNeuron_Test
python NeuronDemo.py
python BallStickDemo.py
python NonIsopotentialCable.py</pre>
<p>The scripts do not depend on each other except that
<span class="code">BallStickDemo.py</span> can optionally import
<span class="code">NeuronMatplotlibVisualizer</span> from
<span class="code">MatplotlibVis.py</span>. All four files use the same
NEURON installation and the same Python environment.</p>

<h2>7. Output artefacts present in the project folder</h2>
<table class="params">
<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Producer</th><th>Format</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="col-param">data.csv</td><td>NeuronDemo.py (optional)</td><td>CSV, no header</td><td>Time / voltage pairs at <span class="code">soma(0.5)</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">data.p</td><td>NeuronDemo.py (optional)</td><td>pickle, binary</td><td>Dict with NEURON Vectors for t and v.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">output.txt</td><td>NeuronDemo.py</td><td>Plain text</td><td>Pretty-printed <span class="code">soma.psection()</span> dictionary.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">plot.png</td><td>NeuronDemo.py</td><td>PNG</td><td>v(t) trace at <span class="code">soma(0.5)</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">neuron_shape_3d.png</td><td>MatplotlibVis.py</td><td>PNG</td><td>3D morphology rendering (per <span class="code">save_path</span>).</td></tr>
<tr><td class="col-param">test_neuron_shape_3d.png</td><td>MatplotlibVis.py</td><td>PNG</td><td>Backup / test rendering of the same view.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p class="muted" style="margin-top:18pt;">End of methods document.</p>
